          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "To find a specific ICD-10 code from a root like K21, you need to follow a few steps:\n\n1. **Understand the Root**: The root code K21 refers to \"Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD)\" in the ICD-10 coding system. Roots often represent broader categories of conditions.\n\n2. **Consult the ICD-10 Manual**: In the ICD-10 manual, look up the root code (K21) to find the range and the specific codes associated with it. The manual will list subcategories under the root.\n\n3. **Identify Specific Codes**: Under K21, you will see specific codes that detail different aspects or variations of the condition, such as K21.0 (Gastro-esophageal reflux disease with esophagitis) and K21.9 (Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis).\n\n4. **Use Code Descriptions**: Read the descriptions of each specific code to determine which one accurately describes the condition you are working with.\n\nThis process ensures that you can accurately identify the specific code that corresponds to the broader root. If you need more detailed information or examples, let me know!\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
